WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.720
We are making music with one
of the most talented people

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:13.090
I've ever met named
Jacob Collier.

00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:16.330
So you go, (SINGING) you
don't wanna be my savior.

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:20.020
And you don't want to--
especially with the--

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:22.000
you don't wanna be my savior.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.420
In the music world,
Jacob's huge, you know?

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:26.110
And so in some
ways, for him to get

00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:28.402
to work with all these
musicians, it's the best thing.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:36.990
We were able to get
him to come to MIT

00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:39.666
and kind of do a
week long residency.

00:00:44.148 --> 00:00:46.560
MIT's visiting artist
program is trying

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:50.850
to bring great artists
in substantive contact

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:52.290
with our students.

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:54.720
It's all about music
education experiences.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:58.690
I wanted them to feel the
sparks of Jacob's creativity

00:00:58.690 --> 00:01:02.850
firsthand, and I wanted
them to go beyond that,

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:06.460
to get inside his music and to
be able to make it their own.

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:08.900
For this kind of thing, it's--
all you really need is--

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:15.665
is like a-- you
know what I mean?

00:01:15.665 --> 00:01:19.924
More than like-- you know,
you could even find like a--

00:01:19.924 --> 00:01:21.465
I mean, I'd do your
own thing but you

00:01:21.465 --> 00:01:23.631
could find a riffy thing,
that's just kind of like--

00:01:26.686 --> 00:01:27.560
you know what I mean?

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:29.850
And just like-- because
I guess the guitar is

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:31.631
like basically a
drummer here as opposed

00:01:31.631 --> 00:01:32.880
to being a harmony instrument.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:34.643
He creates a really
great atmosphere

00:01:34.643 --> 00:01:37.059
for working with people and
bringing the best out of them.

00:01:47.587 --> 00:01:48.420
That sounds amazing.

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:54.100
It's a creative environment,
and that really feeds me.

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:56.200
Their minds are obviously
so sharp, you know?

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:58.450
And it's not even
necessarily musically sharp

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:01.702
in the foreground, but these
guys think about stuff.

00:02:01.702 --> 00:02:04.160
There's this gig on Saturday
that if don't have tickets to,

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:06.410
I believe there's a waiting
list, but you should come.

00:02:06.410 --> 00:02:08.080
It's going to be so crazy--

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:09.280
200 musicians on the stage.

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:16.970
I'm performing with Jacob
Collier, which is amazing.

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:28.250
When I listen to his music,
it like sends chills up

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:29.760
my spine, especially "Hideaway."

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:31.009
Like, that's my favorite song.

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:40.176
A sense of peace
a sense of calm,

00:02:40.176 --> 00:02:41.300
and something making sense.

00:02:53.787 --> 00:02:55.370
When I started
listening to the music,

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:58.400
I realized this was a
lot more complicated

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:00.695
than most arranging projects.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:14.400
As a music nerd, it's a
beautiful looking score to me

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.064
because there's all
these things that

00:03:16.064 --> 00:03:18.480
didn't appear like they should
be there from the beginning

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:20.392
and they start to show up.

00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:37.100
His music is in many ways
like classical music, which

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:41.060
is that so much of
it is specified.

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:51.950
Take me anywhere you want to go.

00:03:54.920 --> 00:04:01.930
You know that my love is strong.

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:06.490
It's pretty moving for some--
well, "Hideaway" especially.

00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:08.080
There are certain
points in the music

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:10.130
where it's pretty overwhelming.

00:04:10.130 --> 00:04:11.630
[MUSIC - JACOB COLLIER,
 "HIDEAWAY"]

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:20.769
(SINGING) Softly, like the
calm that follows storms

00:04:20.769 --> 00:04:22.520
So he states the melody.

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:23.850
He sings the melody.

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:27.120
It's all very tonal and
pretty like a pop song.

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:31.500
And then we get to the
bridge and suddenly he

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:33.000
throws us for a loop.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.316
(SINGING) In my hideaway.

00:04:41.288 --> 00:04:55.232
Even when I close
my eyes, darling I

00:04:55.232 --> 00:05:11.168
will always stay wide
awake in my hideaway.

00:05:17.642 --> 00:05:30.800
Touch me like I've never
loved before in the place

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:40.869
the I adore in my hideaway.

00:05:40.869 --> 00:05:42.910
The drummer has one of
the hardest things of all.

00:05:42.910 --> 00:05:45.400
He's got to play the
fives on the symbol

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:47.570
but still maintain the
slow four with his foot.

00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:57.986
(SINIGING) I know whichever
way the wind may blow,

00:05:57.986 --> 00:06:06.914
there will be a place for
me to go in my hideaway.

00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:31.218
Whether you be lost
or found, darling, I

00:06:31.218 --> 00:06:59.220
will always stay wide awake
in my hideaway, my hideaway.

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:01.620
Down, falling down.

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:03.540
Down, falling down.

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:05.940
Down, falling down.

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:07.860
Down, falling down.

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:10.770
In the second verse, he
adds this layer of fives.

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:12.418
So, 3, 4, 5.

00:07:12.418 --> 00:07:15.038
[TICKING]

00:07:18.387 --> 00:07:19.970
And it's made more
complex by the fact

00:07:19.970 --> 00:07:22.685
that the fives are grouped in
fours and threes and sixes.

00:07:25.445 --> 00:07:42.590
falling down, falling down,
falling down to the ground

00:07:42.590 --> 00:07:45.180
It's-- you know, you don't
hear the math of it when

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:46.760
you're listening to the song.

00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:48.640
You just suddenly
become unmoored

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:50.400
in this kind of dreamlike state.

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:53.442
And yet you still
hear the slow pulse

00:07:53.442 --> 00:07:54.900
from the original
part of the song.

00:07:58.176 --> 00:08:02.280
He's kind of playing with this
notion of you, the listener,

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:04.710
thinking you know where you are.

00:08:04.710 --> 00:08:08.490
And he'll throw something
at you that unmoors it

00:08:08.490 --> 00:08:11.815
but without completely
divorcing you from the structure

00:08:11.815 --> 00:08:12.690
that you knew before.

00:08:30.618 --> 00:08:34.104
[OOING]

00:08:52.032 --> 00:08:54.520
It's just-- it's a revelation.

00:08:54.520 --> 00:08:55.820
It's amazing.

00:08:55.820 --> 00:09:00.310
And all of this is happening
in what at the beginning

00:09:00.310 --> 00:09:03.550
seemed to be a simple
four chord pop song.

00:09:03.550 --> 00:09:07.029
[SCATTING]

00:09:12.854 --> 00:09:14.270
This is kind of
what our lives are

00:09:14.270 --> 00:09:17.360
like is that we try to
reduce them so that we

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:18.740
can get through the day.

00:09:18.740 --> 00:09:22.850
But there's stuff going on that
if we just pay attention to it

00:09:22.850 --> 00:09:26.270
is beautiful,
inspiring, frightening.

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:30.170
And he's kind of encapsulating
all of this in a five or six

00:09:30.170 --> 00:09:33.346
minute song that appears to be
a pop ballad at the beginning.

00:09:33.346 --> 00:09:36.322
[SCATTING]

00:09:37.810 --> 00:09:49.218
Let me feel the sky
and feel the moon.

00:09:49.218 --> 00:09:57.650
Let me sing and always
tune to my hideaway.

00:10:01.170 --> 00:10:03.120
It was just like
everything I had ever

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:08.010
wanted to hear in music because
it's so intricate in detail,

00:10:08.010 --> 00:10:11.430
but it's purposeful, and like,
he has a purpose for everything

00:10:11.430 --> 00:10:15.267
he does, every note he hits,
every weird beat that he plays.

00:10:15.267 --> 00:10:21.728
Find a home in everything
in my hideaway.

00:10:28.686 --> 00:10:39.620
Maybe you can come to stay.

00:10:39.620 --> 00:10:59.500
And I will meet you here
someday far away in my hideaway.

00:11:19.380 --> 00:11:22.330
What my mom did is
recognize that I

00:11:22.330 --> 00:11:25.030
had some kind of weird brain
that was very thirsty and very

00:11:25.030 --> 00:11:28.300
inventive and quite
emotionally mature

00:11:28.300 --> 00:11:31.000
and presented lots of
different things to me.

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:31.990
Like, this is Bach.

00:11:31.990 --> 00:11:32.895
This is Stravinsky.

00:11:32.895 --> 00:11:33.520
This is Bartok.

00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:33.790
This is Britain.

00:11:33.790 --> 00:11:34.470
This is Earth, Wind, and Fire.

00:11:34.470 --> 00:11:35.455
This is Bobby McFerrin.

00:11:35.455 --> 00:11:36.050
This is Sting.

00:11:36.050 --> 00:11:39.100
Those are like a lot of
my heroes growing up.

00:11:39.100 --> 00:11:41.080
And I wasn't told to
do anything with them.

00:11:41.080 --> 00:11:42.330
I was just told to enjoy them.

00:11:42.330 --> 00:11:44.538
She used to do things like
turn on the vacuum cleaner

00:11:44.538 --> 00:11:46.650
or, as we say in the UK,
the Hoover and plug it in

00:11:46.650 --> 00:11:48.760
and it would go [VACUUM SOUND]
and she would say,

00:11:48.760 --> 00:11:50.090
Jacob, what note
does that feel like?

00:11:50.090 --> 00:11:51.610
This is when I'm
two, two years old.

00:11:51.610 --> 00:11:53.180
I was like, G?

00:11:53.180 --> 00:11:55.210
yeah, that's a G.
Really, the biggest gift

00:11:55.210 --> 00:11:58.094
I was given as a child was
a space and the affirmation

00:11:58.094 --> 00:11:58.760
to create in it.

00:11:58.760 --> 00:12:02.600
And I was never, ever
taught to practice ever.

00:12:02.600 --> 00:12:03.910
I was never taught to practice.

00:12:03.910 --> 00:12:05.160
And people don't believe that.

00:12:05.160 --> 00:12:06.390
No, no way.

00:12:06.390 --> 00:12:07.790
where's the discipline?

00:12:07.790 --> 00:12:10.660
Sort of thing, and it's
funny because it's true,

00:12:10.660 --> 00:12:13.170
but people have an
idea of how to learn

00:12:13.170 --> 00:12:16.912
and how to teach that I think
is horrifically out of date.

00:12:16.912 --> 00:12:24.188
Do my dreaming and my scheming.

00:12:24.188 --> 00:12:28.012
Lie awake and pray.

00:12:31.760 --> 00:12:39.035
Do my crying and my sighing.

00:12:39.035 --> 00:12:41.660
Laugh at yesterday.

00:12:47.588 --> 00:12:53.022
Now it's dark and I'm alone.

00:12:53.022 --> 00:13:11.300
But I won't be afraid in my
room, in my room, in my room,

00:13:11.300 --> 00:13:15.009
in my room.

00:13:15.009 --> 00:13:17.050
I like the idea of at the
beginning of "Hajanga,"

00:13:17.050 --> 00:13:18.570
the first thing you hear
is the strings come in.

00:13:18.570 --> 00:13:20.040
It's like the announcing
of a new song.

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:20.450
Yeah.

00:13:20.450 --> 00:13:21.850
So I quite like the change
of texture for that.

00:13:21.850 --> 00:13:22.350
That's cool.

00:13:22.350 --> 00:13:23.760
Do you want to do those edits?

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:25.760
You can plug in the
speakers and do those edits.

00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:26.970
I'd love that, yeah.

00:13:26.970 --> 00:13:28.210
Can we plug in a microphone?

00:13:28.210 --> 00:13:28.710
Yeah.

00:13:28.710 --> 00:13:29.260
Ah, sweet.

00:13:29.260 --> 00:13:32.424
[SINGING]

00:13:33.880 --> 00:13:36.240
I saw one of his
videos on YouTube

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:38.460
and I thought, oh my
gosh, this guy's amazing.

00:13:38.460 --> 00:13:41.640
He's got this great sense of
aesthetics and editing skills

00:13:41.640 --> 00:13:43.474
and mixing and all these things.

00:13:43.474 --> 00:13:45.015
And I like to build
music tech stuff.

00:13:45.015 --> 00:13:47.410
And so I sent him a
Facebook message saying--

00:13:47.410 --> 00:13:48.980
I'm at MIT and I make stuff.

00:13:48.980 --> 00:13:50.938
Have you any ideas of
stuff you'd like to make?

00:13:50.938 --> 00:13:52.182
And I said, oh, yes I have.

00:13:52.182 --> 00:13:54.432
I've always dreamed of
singing harmony on the spot,

00:13:54.432 --> 00:13:56.140
be able to essentially
improvise a choir.

00:13:56.140 --> 00:13:58.350
Singing in the life time.

00:13:58.350 --> 00:14:02.900
Take these broken wings
and learn to fly, oh, oh.

00:14:02.900 --> 00:14:05.820
A harmonizer takes
whatever you're singing.

00:14:05.820 --> 00:14:08.280
And there's a keyboard and
you can play different notes.

00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:10.710
And the notes that you
play are transformed

00:14:10.710 --> 00:14:14.270
so that what you sing is spread
across lots and lots of notes--

00:14:14.270 --> 00:14:18.210
basically multiplies
his voice up in stacks.

00:14:18.210 --> 00:14:23.850
Take these broken
wings and learn to fly.

00:14:23.850 --> 00:14:29.522
Oh, all your life
you were only--

00:14:29.522 --> 00:14:33.996
you were only waiting for this
moment to-- moment to be free.

00:14:33.996 --> 00:14:38.926
Blackbird, fly.

00:14:38.926 --> 00:14:42.377
Blackbird, fly.

00:14:50.760 --> 00:14:52.679
Because this is like
woodwind and then sax.

00:14:52.679 --> 00:14:54.220
Those sounds go
together, don't they?

00:14:54.220 --> 00:14:54.920
Yeah.

00:14:54.920 --> 00:14:57.950
I'm going to be creating this
kind of improvised harmonic,

00:14:57.950 --> 00:15:02.470
more spontaneous technological
musical compound piece where

00:15:02.470 --> 00:15:04.550
I can send the
musicians information

00:15:04.550 --> 00:15:07.590
during the performance via
maybe iPad screens or something

00:15:07.590 --> 00:15:09.602
like this where I say,
you play these notes now.

00:15:09.602 --> 00:15:10.310
And they play it.

00:15:10.310 --> 00:15:11.510
It swells up and swells down.

00:15:11.510 --> 00:15:12.843
And then I can fill in the gaps.

00:15:12.843 --> 00:15:14.980
And it's like a
big conversation.

00:15:14.980 --> 00:15:16.730
And this is one of the
main pieces of work

00:15:16.730 --> 00:15:18.740
that will be done
between now and December.

00:15:18.740 --> 00:15:22.520
I think on stage,
you'll need one laptop.

00:15:22.520 --> 00:15:24.271
That will be the MIDI input.

00:15:24.271 --> 00:15:24.770
OK.

00:15:24.770 --> 00:15:26.020
And that will have a keyboard.

00:15:38.520 --> 00:15:45.160
We each open up a web page on
our phone, and there's a staff.

00:15:45.160 --> 00:15:47.920
So he's picking all these
different notes, chords,

00:15:47.920 --> 00:15:51.100
rhythms, everything for
every different instrument.

00:15:51.100 --> 00:15:54.370
And we're playing them as
they come up on our screen.

00:15:54.370 --> 00:15:58.016
It's allowing Jacob to literally
play the band in real time.

00:16:16.370 --> 00:16:17.052
I'm a scientist.

00:16:17.052 --> 00:16:18.760
I'm an engineer too,
so I like to tinker.

00:16:18.760 --> 00:16:20.430
I like to explore things.

00:16:20.430 --> 00:16:24.280
And one of my best
laboratories is with music.

00:16:24.280 --> 00:16:26.910
So I think if anything
Jacob, has made us

00:16:26.910 --> 00:16:31.890
into bolder, more risk
taking experimentalists.

00:16:31.890 --> 00:16:34.440
I learned perhaps more
about how it's best

00:16:34.440 --> 00:16:37.965
not to learn and not to
teach than how to learn

00:16:37.965 --> 00:16:38.830
and how to teach.

00:16:38.830 --> 00:16:41.070
Don't give people all
the answers to things.

00:16:41.070 --> 00:16:42.528
If you give people
all the answers,

00:16:42.528 --> 00:16:44.320
then they are the
subject to your criteria.

00:16:44.320 --> 00:16:46.528
But if you give them all
the questions and the clues,

00:16:46.528 --> 00:16:48.150
then they find
their own answers.

00:16:48.150 --> 00:16:51.720
And actually, the process of
finding an answer is almost all

00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:52.442
of learning.

00:16:52.442 --> 00:16:54.150
So I think teachers
have a responsibility

00:16:54.150 --> 00:16:57.484
to trust their students
to find not only answers,

00:16:57.484 --> 00:17:00.150
but their own answers that might
be different from the teacher's

00:17:00.150 --> 00:17:02.066
answers, especially when
it comes to something

00:17:02.066 --> 00:17:05.369
like jazz which is so vague
and so expressive and personal.

00:17:05.369 --> 00:17:08.734
(SINGING) If I let
your mind create me,

00:17:08.734 --> 00:17:12.192
the work will never end.

00:17:12.192 --> 00:17:15.156
I don't wanna be a preacher.

00:17:15.156 --> 00:17:18.120
I just wanna be a man.

00:17:18.120 --> 00:17:21.084
I won't let you exaltate me.

00:17:21.084 --> 00:17:23.554
Just to keep me as I am.

00:17:23.554 --> 00:17:26.518
I don't wanna be a---

00:17:26.518 --> 00:17:29.482
We have to take
the cue from him.

00:17:29.482 --> 00:17:31.050
I had an idea for like--

00:17:31.050 --> 00:17:33.870
for the section that
it goes [VOCALIZING]..

00:17:33.870 --> 00:17:35.650
I could be there like
with the [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:35.650 --> 00:17:36.580
I'm getting stuff.

00:17:36.580 --> 00:17:39.370
[VOCALIZING]

00:17:40.770 --> 00:17:42.432
Just to give them
the rhythm there.

00:17:42.432 --> 00:17:43.818
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:17:43.818 --> 00:17:45.585
[INAUDIBLE]

00:17:45.585 --> 00:17:46.084
OK.

00:17:50.132 --> 00:17:51.590
At what stage in
the process do you

00:17:51.590 --> 00:17:53.200
write the lyrics to the song?

00:17:53.200 --> 00:17:53.700
It depends.

00:17:53.700 --> 00:17:55.260
Sometimes they're the
first thing that I write.

00:17:55.260 --> 00:17:55.980
I write a lot.

00:17:55.980 --> 00:17:57.563
I write-- I have a
thought book that's

00:17:57.563 --> 00:17:59.580
filled with words,
really dense imagery,

00:17:59.580 --> 00:18:01.650
like really interesting,
dense imagery.

00:18:01.650 --> 00:18:02.520
And when I come to write lyrics.

00:18:02.520 --> 00:18:04.020
I try and write as
little of it as I

00:18:04.020 --> 00:18:06.240
can because the music
is already complex

00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:09.004
and I need to allow
people to find

00:18:09.004 --> 00:18:10.170
their meanings in the words.

00:18:10.170 --> 00:18:11.920
Like the song "Hajanga"
it's just a number

00:18:11.920 --> 00:18:13.260
of seven syllable lines.

00:18:13.260 --> 00:18:16.230
Everybody near and far,
come together as you are.

00:18:16.230 --> 00:18:18.980
And to the ocean, to the sky,
sing that cosmic lullaby.

00:18:18.980 --> 00:18:20.730
That could mean lots
of different things.

00:18:20.730 --> 00:18:21.945
That could mean life and death.

00:18:21.945 --> 00:18:24.000
It could mean a simple
song that your mother sings

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:24.720
to you before you go to sleep.

00:18:24.720 --> 00:18:26.660
It could mean the
actually universe-- like,

00:18:26.660 --> 00:18:27.701
lots of different things.

00:18:36.180 --> 00:18:38.160
I'd rather write words
that invite people

00:18:38.160 --> 00:18:40.640
to understand them rather
than project my understanding

00:18:40.640 --> 00:18:41.390
onto other people.

00:19:06.632 --> 00:19:17.930
Everybody near and far
come together as you are.

00:19:21.845 --> 00:19:33.060
And to the ocean, to the sky,
sing that cosmic lullaby.

00:19:33.060 --> 00:19:35.268
Sing that lullaby.

00:19:35.268 --> 00:19:36.744
Sing the hajanga.

00:19:46.584 --> 00:19:55.011
Sing your pleasure,
sing your pain

00:19:55.011 --> 00:19:57.506
like I'll never sing again.

00:20:01.498 --> 00:20:09.570
Let it echo loud
and clear across

00:20:09.570 --> 00:20:11.963
the ancient stratosphere.

00:20:17.144 --> 00:20:24.090
And when the good
days pass you by,

00:20:24.090 --> 00:20:28.304
there's a spark of
joy that can be found.

00:20:31.671 --> 00:20:40.430
And when those dark clouds bring
you down lift your hands up

00:20:40.430 --> 00:20:42.310
from the sky singing

00:20:42.310 --> 00:20:45.286
[SCATTING]

00:21:12.566 --> 00:21:20.130
As the words go round and
round and round and round,

00:21:20.130 --> 00:21:23.850
let the tears roll
down and down.

00:21:23.850 --> 00:21:26.640
[SCATTING]

00:21:27.140 --> 00:21:34.960
Sing the way you
wish to be across

00:21:34.960 --> 00:21:37.420
the ancient stratosphere.

00:21:42.832 --> 00:21:48.954
Even when those dark
clouds bring you down,

00:21:48.954 --> 00:21:55.732
there's a spark of
joy that can be found.

00:21:55.732 --> 00:22:01.564
Oh, even when things
break and fall apart.

00:22:04.480 --> 00:22:08.446
Lift your voices from
your heart singing--

00:22:08.446 --> 00:22:11.338
[SCATTING]

00:22:18.568 --> 00:22:21.780
So every morning
and every night,

00:22:21.780 --> 00:22:30.322
from that darkness to the light,
like a beacon shining bright,

00:22:30.322 --> 00:22:31.795
sing the hajanga.

00:22:31.795 --> 00:22:33.030
You'll be all right.

00:22:33.030 --> 00:22:36.818
From the winter
comes the spring.

00:22:36.818 --> 00:22:41.076
It don't matter
what life may bring.

00:22:41.076 --> 00:22:44.569
Said you can do most anything.

00:22:44.569 --> 00:22:48.561
So give your hajanga
a song to sing.

00:22:48.561 --> 00:22:51.555
So tell your mama
and tell your pa.

00:22:51.555 --> 00:22:55.547
Sing it near and sing it far.

00:22:55.547 --> 00:23:00.038
Be exactly the way you are.

00:23:00.038 --> 00:23:01.535
See the hajanga.

00:23:01.535 --> 00:23:03.531
It's your guiding star.

00:23:03.531 --> 00:23:10.018
So every woman, every man, in
every nation, in every land,

00:23:10.018 --> 00:23:14.010
I said please you've
got to understand.

00:23:14.010 --> 00:23:18.002
Now sing the hajanga
and take my hand.

00:23:42.453 --> 00:23:45.532
Then one day, your
life is through.

00:23:49.690 --> 00:23:53.000
Nothing more that you can do.

00:23:56.792 --> 00:24:03.999
So give away the things
you know, things you know.

00:24:03.999 --> 00:24:10.957
And tell your friends
you love them so.

00:24:10.957 --> 00:24:12.448
Tell them hajanga.

00:25:02.984 --> 00:25:04.900
Probably busy this whole
week, but if you ever

00:25:04.900 --> 00:25:07.599
find like an hour and you want
to go grab a pint anywhere--

00:25:07.599 --> 00:25:08.515
yeah, yeah, fantastic.

00:25:08.515 --> 00:25:09.850
Are you're going-- you
coming on Saturday?

00:25:09.850 --> 00:25:10.351
I am.

00:25:10.351 --> 00:25:12.349
Just find me after the--
find me after the show.

00:25:12.349 --> 00:25:13.194
I'll be hanging out.

00:25:13.194 --> 00:25:13.636
Cool, all right.

00:25:13.636 --> 00:25:14.135
Fantastic.

00:25:14.135 --> 00:25:14.690
All right.

00:25:14.690 --> 00:25:14.840
So--

00:25:15.090 --> 00:25:16.170
Yeah, yeah, so nice
to meet you too.

00:25:16.170 --> 00:25:16.810
Yeah, see you soon.

00:25:16.810 --> 00:25:17.309
Thank you.

00:25:17.309 --> 00:25:19.530
Cheers, see you later.

00:25:19.530 --> 00:25:24.250
I really feel like MIT is a sort
of second home in some ways.

00:25:24.250 --> 00:25:26.320
I've always felt
some kind of kinship

00:25:26.320 --> 00:25:29.390
with this kind of idea of
celebrating the introvert.

00:25:29.390 --> 00:25:32.830
At MIT, it's a sort of sublime
understanding of the people

00:25:32.830 --> 00:25:33.965
who think inwards first.

00:25:33.965 --> 00:25:36.740
And I would count myself in
amongst that group of people.

00:25:36.740 --> 00:25:40.589
So I'd like to celebrate that
idea, I think, and being at MIT

00:25:40.589 --> 00:25:42.130
consistently reminds
me how wonderful

00:25:42.130 --> 00:25:45.190
it is when people
think beyond this level

00:25:45.190 --> 00:25:47.660
and up and down into the
other realms of things.

00:25:47.660 --> 00:25:51.580
So I'd like to just sort of say
thank you to MIT as a concept.

00:25:59.924 --> 00:26:11.930
[INAUDIBLE]

00:26:11.930 --> 00:26:14.870
If the groove's
going [BEAT BOXING],,

00:26:14.870 --> 00:26:20.220
and the bass is going
[BEAT BOXING WITH BASS NOTES],,

00:26:20.220 --> 00:26:21.240
that's cool, right?

00:26:21.240 --> 00:26:24.300
It's like, it's more cool
than [BASIC BASS LINE]..

00:26:24.300 --> 00:26:27.940
Yeah, [COMPLEX BEAT BOXING],,
because they

00:26:27.940 --> 00:26:29.940
have a gravity that goes
towards the same place,

00:26:29.940 --> 00:26:31.650
but they don't
have a unison line.

00:26:31.650 --> 00:26:32.880
Let's try a different groove.

00:26:32.880 --> 00:26:34.470
This is a groove
that's hard to nail,

00:26:34.470 --> 00:26:36.250
but it's the groove
on the record.

00:26:36.250 --> 00:26:38.130
And it's like a lilt groove.

00:26:38.130 --> 00:26:40.711
So it's less of a shuffle.

00:26:40.711 --> 00:26:43.210
And it's like halfway between
a shuffle and a normal groove.

00:26:43.210 --> 00:26:48.170
So this is straight, and
then this is triplet.

00:26:48.170 --> 00:26:52.280
It's like halfway
between like this.

00:26:52.280 --> 00:26:54.379
It's less about putting
the high hat later.

00:26:54.379 --> 00:26:56.170
It's more about bringing
the snare earlier.

00:26:56.170 --> 00:26:56.753
So it's like--

00:26:56.753 --> 00:27:00.840
[BEAT BOXING]

00:27:00.840 --> 00:27:03.714
Actually, if I play and
then you guys can join in,

00:27:03.714 --> 00:27:05.380
maybe that's a good
way to get the feel.

00:27:05.380 --> 00:27:05.963
So it's like--

00:27:32.930 --> 00:27:34.517
I dig it.

00:27:34.517 --> 00:27:35.850
Can I have a go at the drum kit?

00:27:35.850 --> 00:27:39.770
[INAUDIBLE]

00:27:39.770 --> 00:27:42.710
[PLAYING DRUMS]

00:27:54.960 --> 00:27:58.460
He very carefully and
deliberately swings it

00:27:58.460 --> 00:28:00.760
in certain ways and
modulates the time

00:28:00.760 --> 00:28:02.290
and pulls and
pushes in a way that

00:28:02.290 --> 00:28:06.270
just gets this like
really visceral groove

00:28:06.270 --> 00:28:08.920
where you just like
get into it so much.

00:28:08.920 --> 00:28:12.730
[SINGING]

00:28:12.730 --> 00:28:15.850
That little bit of extra
effort to, you know, change it

00:28:15.850 --> 00:28:17.780
makes such a difference.

00:28:17.780 --> 00:28:20.695
And that's such a Jacob
kind of construct.

00:28:20.695 --> 00:28:22.980
It's like that extra
bit of intention

00:28:22.980 --> 00:28:25.320
that just-- you know, even
just a tiny little breath

00:28:25.320 --> 00:28:28.265
or a tiny little extra note or
something that doesn't quite,

00:28:28.265 --> 00:28:31.770
you know, fit in what the rules
say they're supposed to do.

00:28:31.770 --> 00:28:33.509
So at the end, were you guys--

00:28:33.509 --> 00:28:35.050
had you guys split
into two sections,

00:28:35.050 --> 00:28:36.210
or were you singing
different things?

00:28:36.210 --> 00:28:38.520
Some of them saying, I don't
want to be [INAUDIBLE]..

00:28:38.520 --> 00:28:39.444
And the other one was going--

00:28:39.444 --> 00:28:39.910
You don't want to be--

00:28:39.910 --> 00:28:40.250
Yeah.

00:28:40.250 --> 00:28:40.780
Yeah, yeah.

00:28:40.780 --> 00:28:42.180
We could do girls versus boys.

00:28:42.180 --> 00:28:44.425
It could be so like,
I don't want to be,

00:28:44.425 --> 00:28:46.800
and then, you don't want to
be my-- you know what I mean?

00:28:46.800 --> 00:28:48.174
Put your hand in
the air if you'd

00:28:48.174 --> 00:28:51.040
be willing to sing along
in this next song with me.

00:28:51.040 --> 00:28:54.070
And by that, I mean
everybody raise your hand.

00:28:54.070 --> 00:28:55.597
This is a song called "Savior."

00:28:55.597 --> 00:28:56.930
It's the last tune of the night.

00:28:56.930 --> 00:28:58.305
Thank you so, so
much for coming.

00:28:58.305 --> 00:28:59.890
This must be what
Quincy Jones felt

00:28:59.890 --> 00:29:03.160
when he first encountered Stevie
Wonder or Michael Jackson--

00:29:03.160 --> 00:29:06.400
that here's someone
who has really, really

00:29:06.400 --> 00:29:08.050
